module strategy.year_end_plan

import std.types { Int, List, NonEmptyStr, Bool }

// THE PLAN FROM 2026-08-29 TO YEAR END (operator direction 2026-08-29), worked BACKWARDS
// from a small private demo - the current 4 servers, free founding credits to a handful of
// trusted customers - with every long-lead lever (compliance, security, storage, colo
// agreements, acquisition instrumentation, machine health) sorted BEFORE the exponential-
// scaling window can demand it. AMENDED 2026-08-29 per the external analyst's ruling: the
// three anchor DATES stand, but their exits are recut so each date establishes the
// smallest executed fact that safely authorizes the next irreversible step, never the
// largest plausible feature bundle attachable to the date; two internal stage gates
// (2026-10-15, 2026-11-23) keep the outer anchors honest about what they prove.

// M4JanuaryLaunch ADDED 2026-09-07 (operator: "I meant to say January 7"). The plan previously
// terminated at the year-end gate, which made the launch reachable only in prose while the plan's
// own arithmetic reported the graph finished. 2027-01-07 is day 131 on the plan epoch, nineteen
// days past M3 -- and that spread is the whole schedule for whichever launch form is chosen, which
// is why strategy.january_launch reads BOTH representations from here rather than restating the
// date: one anchor, one authority.
type PlanMilestone
  = M1InternalVertical
  | M1bInternalS0Proof
  | M2FirstExternal
  | M2bPrivateCohort
  | M3YearEndGate
  | M4JanuaryLaunch

fn milestone_order(m: PlanMilestone) -> Int {
  match m {
    M1InternalVertical => 1,
    M1bInternalS0Proof => 2,
    M2FirstExternal => 3,
    M2bPrivateCohort => 4,
    M3YearEndGate => 5,
    M4JanuaryLaunch => 6,
  }
}

fn milestone_anchor_date(m: PlanMilestone) -> NonEmptyStr {
  match m {
    M1InternalVertical => "2026-10-01",
    M1bInternalS0Proof => "2026-10-15",
    M2FirstExternal => "2026-11-02",
    M2bPrivateCohort => "2026-11-23",
    M3YearEndGate => "2026-12-19",
    M4JanuaryLaunch => "2027-01-07",
  }
}

// Day offsets from plan epoch 2026-08-29 - the arithmetic carrier for schedule math (ISO
// strings compare but do not subtract). Both representations are declared per milestone
// and the witness asserts they order together.
fn milestone_anchor_day(m: PlanMilestone) -> Int {
  match m {
    M1InternalVertical => 33,
    M1bInternalS0Proof => 47,
    M2FirstExternal => 65,
    M2bPrivateCohort => 86,
    M3YearEndGate => 112,
    M4JanuaryLaunch => 131,
  }
}

type PlanWorkstream
  = Hardware
  | StorageFabric
  | RunnerSoftware
  | SecurityCompliance
  | AcquisitionMarketing
  | OpsMachineHealth
  | EconomicsQa
  | PeopleAndProcess

// THE SINGLE-OPERATOR CORRECTION (analyst): seven workstreams are NOT seven parallel
// lanes - hardware handling, security judgment, legal review, customer onboarding,
// commercial decisions and incident ownership all converge on one person. Every item
// declares how it consumes the operator; the honest WIP shape is roughly one primary
// OperatorExclusive build item plus concurrent ExternalLead queues at a time.
type ExecutionMode
  = OperatorExclusive
  | AgentParallel
  | ExternalLead
  | CalendarObservation

type PlanItemStatus
  = NotStarted
  | InProgress { note: NonEmptyStr }
  | Done { receipt: NonEmptyStr }
  | DeclaredBehind { reason: NonEmptyStr }

// rank: a declared topological level - every dependency must carry a STRICTLY smaller
// rank, which makes a dependency cycle unwritable (no cycle admits strictly decreasing
// ranks). Construction standing where the previous witness only checked resolution.
// min_calendar_days: the item's floor duration; latest safe start derives from it.
type PlanItem {
  id: NonEmptyStr
  workstream: PlanWorkstream
  milestone: PlanMilestone
  rank: Int
  min_calendar_days: Int
  execution_mode: ExecutionMode
  exit_criterion: NonEmptyStr
  depends_on: List<NonEmptyStr>
  relates_to: List<NonEmptyStr>
  status: PlanItemStatus
}

data no_deps: List<NonEmptyStr> = []

data no_refs: List<NonEmptyStr> = []

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// M1 - INTERNAL VERTICAL (2026-10-01): ONE qualified server accepts a real GitHub Actions
// job through the App/webhook path, executes it isolated and ephemeral against M0 CAS with
// durable commit, meters it, destroys the environment. Dogfood BEGINS. (Recut from
// "4 servers + production netboot + 2 weeks dogfood complete", which required all four
// predecessors done by ~Sep 17 - not honest with 20 of 21 items NotStarted on Aug 29.)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

data item_hw_first_host: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "hw-first-host",
  workstream: Hardware,
  milestone: M1InternalVertical,
  rank: 10,
  min_calendar_days: 14,
  execution_mode: OperatorExclusive,
  exit_criterion: "Unit 1 through intake phases 1-5: FRU'd, BMC recredentialed, memtest pass, Linux EDAC pass clean (nproc 160), DIMM population matches the J-slot table - one held-unit receipt module. ONE admitted host is the M1 canary; the other three do not gate the vertical.",
  depends_on: no_deps,
  relates_to: ["strategy.mt_collins_held_unit", "strategy.ci_node_build"],
  status: InProgress { note: "FRU'd, BMC probed, memtest PASSED. Route to the remaining phases is PXE netboot rather than a boot drive - the operator already has PXE install working and the unit is driveless - which gets nproc 160, EDAC and the dmidecode per-slot channel map in ONE session rather than three. Outstanding: BMC recredential, EDAC pass, and the J-slot census that decides whether filling the empty DIMM slots raises capacity at the cost of data rate or doubles memory bandwidth for free." },
}

data item_boot_minimum: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "boot-minimum",
  workstream: Hardware,
  milestone: M1InternalVertical,
  rank: 20,
  min_calendar_days: 5,
  execution_mode: AgentParallel,
  exit_criterion: "The M1 boot property WITHOUT the full netboot product: deterministic digest-identified host image, no durable customer state on the boot device, BMC-driven unattended reinstall/reset with a measured host-reset receipt, short-lived runtime credentials, boot device treated as disposable. Production diskless netboot moves to netboot-m0 (M2b) - it can slip without invalidating the demo.",
  depends_on: ["hw-first-host"],
  relates_to: ["strategy.mobo_x_architecture"],
  status: InProgress { note: "Transport PROVEN, boot-minimum NOT closed. Observed on the real machine: boots Linux over PXE (nproc 160, EDAC clean, dmidecode -t memory); the repo-built stateless host image boots; switch_root to tmpfs gives a RAM-backed runtime root; the stock Firecracker jailer is traversed and runs as uid 1000 in a chroot plus namespaces; /dev/kvm opens from inside the jail and a guest kernel is reached under KVM; the host deadline fires, nothing survives, the jail is removed. Receipts in gunbc#10733, on main at c9ef79fdeea. TWO CONJUNCTS REMAIN and neither is transport: (1) BMC-driven unattended reinstall with a MEASURED RESET RECEIPT -- the model merged as gunbc#10739 carrying 51 witnesses but has NEVER EXECUTED, because it needs a pinned BMC credential reachable from fleet converge, an open operator decision; (2) root mount identity before and after switch_root, where the claim is still ahead of its evidence. Production diskless netboot stays with netboot-m0: PXE is present and UNANSWERED on the unit (PXE-E16, no valid offer), so network boot is blocked on infrastructure this fleet has not stood up rather than on the machine. 2026-09-10: gunbc#10630 MERGED, and it changes the BOOT ROUTE rather than either remaining conjunct. Fetching the image, verifying its digest, publishing it into the export, attaching it as virtual media through the MegaRAC controller and handing off the boot device are now modeled operations with typed refusals, executed against the live controller -- so getting a bare unit to a booted state stops being hand-driven. Two things it deliberately does NOT do: it is DISKLESS, NOT RAM-RESIDENT (casper toram is absent from the observed command line, so the read-only layer is read from the presented media on demand, which decides whether the host depends on the boot server for as long as it runs); and it does not touch PXE, so netboot-m0's PXE-E16 blocker stands untouched. The two conjuncts above are unchanged: the reset receipt still needs its pinned BMC credential, and root mount identity across switch_root is still ahead of its evidence." },
}

// ADDED 2026-09-07 on operator instruction. This requirement was live and being tracked OUTSIDE
// the plan -- it sat on the operator's own working list as "no accounts yet, create ASAP" while
// fabric-m0 declared no dependencies and therefore read as startable. That is exactly what
// requirement_coalescence_note forbids: a requirement discovered later is added HERE as an item,
// never tracked in a side list, because an item the plan cannot see cannot be late. Two providers
// rather than one is not redundancy -- the durable origin's whole claim is a failure domain
// disjoint from the worker, and a single provider account is a single point of both failure and
// commercial leverage. It is OperatorExclusive because payment instrument, legal entity and
// region are the operator's to bind, and because none of that is delegable to an agent.
// ADDED 2026-09-07 on operator instruction ("we need buyer agents ... there is no deterministic
// filtering on ebay, too many wrong accounts"), then NARROWED the same day by the operator to the
// scope this row now carries: scan the API listings, produce a sheet for manual review, and commit
// a typed intent to buy after that review. The narrowing is what makes this a two-week item rather
// than a programme -- with a human reading the sheet, the agent does not have to DECIDE fit, only
// to SURFACE the evidence, and deciding fit from unreliable listings was the hard half.
//
// IT INHERITS THE EXISTING PIPELINE RATHER THAN RESTARTING IT. strategy.buyers_pipeline already
// carries DemandRow, ListingObservation, propose(), the viability ceiling and the verified-depth
// refusal, and its scout contract already rules that THE SCOUT NEVER ACTUATES A PURCHASE. This row
// adds the three things that contract names but nothing produces: a cited upstream to read from, a
// projection the operator can actually review, and a typed intent that survives the review.
//
// THE FAILURE THIS MUST NOT REPRODUCE, because a spreadsheet is where it hides best: an attribute
// the listing never stated must not reach the sheet as a blank cell, because a blank reads as
// nothing-to-see and an unstated ECC field is exactly the cell the operator needs to stop on. An
// absence rendered as a value is the same defect the cost-account and actuator-selection lanes were
// closing this week, and a review sheet is its most natural habitat.
data item_buyer_agent_pipeline: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "buyer-agent-pipeline",
  workstream: Hardware,
  milestone: M1bInternalS0Proof,
  rank: 10,
  min_calendar_days: 14,
  execution_mode: AgentParallel,
  exit_criterion: "SCAN, SHEET, INTENT - and no actuation. (1) The marketplace is a cited upstream with its own extdeps module under the external-decomposition rule: API version, the endpoints read, the published rate limits, and the fields a listing payload ACTUALLY returns, so the sheet's columns are derived from the API's response shape rather than invented. (2) A scheduled run turns those responses into ListingObservation rows against buyer_demand_rows and projects them to a review sheet GENERATED FROM THE MODEL, never hand-assembled - a hand-authored projection of something the model can emit is the tell that the model is not the authority. The sheet ranks by the spread between delivered unit price and the row's viability ceiling, and every compatibility attribute renders three-valued: stated and corroborated by part number or photograph, stated only, or ABSENT AND SAID TO BE ABSENT. A blank cell for an unstated attribute is the defect, not the format. (3) After the operator reviews, a PurchaseIntent is COMMITTED TO THE CORPUS as a typed row - demand row, listing identity, units, delivered unit price, the attributes accepted on the operator's judgement rather than on evidence, and the intake obligation it triggers - so what was decided is readable later rather than living in a chat message. NOT owed and deliberately so: purchase actuation, which the scout contract keeps human by design; any scraping, since the agent reads the published API as a signed-in buyer; and automatic fit adjudication, which the manual review replaces for now and which becomes owed only if sheet volume makes reading every row impractical.",
  depends_on: no_deps,
  relates_to: ["strategy.buyers_pipeline", "strategy.procurement_order", "strategy.ci_node_build"],
  status: NotStarted,
}

data item_storage_provider_accounts: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "storage-provider-accounts",
  workstream: StorageFabric,
  milestone: M1InternalVertical,
  rank: 5,
  min_calendar_days: 3,
  execution_mode: OperatorExclusive,
  exit_criterion: "Accounts live at BOTH candidate object-storage providers (Cloudflare R2 and Backblaze B2), American regions, billed to the LLC. Each provider gets its OWN extdeps module under the external-decomposition rule - one independently versioned upstream, one authority, no generic hub enumerating providers - carrying its cited pricing units (storage per GB-month, class A/B operations, egress) and its published quota and rate limits, each with the source URL and the date read. The bucket IDENTITY and the ENROLLED SCOPE are declared here, not left implicit: which bucket is the durable origin, what is admitted to it, and under whose credential. NOT owed by this item: choosing between the two, which is a cost decision the pricing modules make derivable rather than a preference to be asserted now.",
  depends_on: no_deps,
  relates_to: ["strategy.compute_fabric_model", "strategy.ci_storage_tiering"],
  status: InProgress { note: "2026-09-10: ACCOUNTS EXIST AT BOTH PROVIDERS and the Cloudflare half is measured rather than assumed. Account 5f71db675ad3e94c16a3d5c159d16298; a bootstrap API token is in fleet custody at version 1 of cloudflare-account-api-token-bootstrap, pinned to that exact version rather than latest. Read LIVE from the account through the modeled path on 2026-09-10: the bootstrap token carries Account API Tokens Write, which is the group the mint model was written for; and the WRITE gap is closed with an OBSERVED id rather than a guessed one - Workers R2 Storage Bucket Item Write, bucket-scoped, so a durable origin CAN put. The read id already pinned in the public repo verified correct against the same listing. Backblaze account/key 6f38e5a145da. OUTSTANDING before Done: the two extdeps modules, the bucket identity and enrolled scope, and the executed token mint all sit on gunbc#10925, which is UNMERGED and being restructured after a correct request-changes (its bucket roster was a clone of the hostname roster). B2 published rates stay UNAVAILABLE - nothing observed retires that. This item is not Done while the declarations live only on a branch." },
}

data item_fabric_m0: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "fabric-m0",
  workstream: StorageFabric,
  milestone: M1InternalVertical,
  rank: 10,
  min_calendar_days: 14,
  execution_mode: AgentParallel,
  exit_criterion: "M0 fabric per the reviewed sequence: per-node disposable CAS cache, one zero-egress durable origin, digest verification on fill, hit/miss/bytes receipts flowing, durable-commit gate (job success only after required artifact/receipt commit). No peer mesh.",
  depends_on: ["storage-provider-accounts"],
  relates_to: ["strategy.compute_fabric_model"],
  status: InProgress { note: "2026-09-10 REFRESH -- the previous note said gunbc#10641 was open in draft. IT MERGED 2026-09-08, and planning from the stale note would have rebuilt what exists. LANDED: the durable-COMPLETION authority (gunbc.fabric_m0_completion admit_job_publication, total over four outcomes, with F0..F6 as gate predicates and 14 mutation-controlled witnesses). Its ordering is the load-bearing part -- the computation verdict is read FIRST and short-circuits, so a successful storage finalization CANNOT invert a failed build, and ReadbackUnknown is a THIRD arm because a lost acknowledgement does not establish that a write failed and an object existing does not establish it is the intended object. Also landed: gunbc#10687, the event-log root path authority that refuses on an unknown host rather than defaulting. REMAINING, AND THE TWO-TRACK SEQUENCING THIS NOTE CARRIED ON 2026-09-10 WAS WRONG. It said giving admit_job_publication a production consumer needed no origin and no cache, so it could run in parallel with the R2 mint. The fabric-M0 lane refuted that by tracing the real run path rather than by argument: the required-CI job ends at claim_executor --required-ci, where phases accumulate failures and the process exit maps to the GitHub check through floor_exit_to_check_conclusion, and NOTHING on that path constructs a CommitTransportOutcome or a ManifestReadbackObservation. So that path cannot ask admit_job_publication without one of two forbidden moves -- fabricating an origin readback, which is the laundering this item's own ceiling calls out and which is the sibling A1's whole subject, or refusing every green job. (1) THE DURABLE-COMMIT GATE IS THEREFORE GATED on the executed R2 token mint, exactly as the origin binding is. (2) SO IS the origin binding itself, then digest verification on fill. THE ONLY GENUINELY UNBLOCKED WORK IS origin-readback discipline as a construction, which is in flight. THE SCHEDULE CONSEQUENCE IS THE POINT: the executed mint is now the SINGLE critical-path item for this item, not a dependency of one half, so it slips the whole milestone rather than part of it. And the missing capability is named rather than the artifact: a job-ending that already possesses the run's Work, the attempt's commit transport, and an INDEPENDENT origin readback of the commit manifest, deciding the check conclusion from the returned JobPublication. (3) ONLY THEN the per-node disposable CAS cache -- no RAID, no scrub, no rebuild, media telemetry plus auto-cull plus spare pool -- and the hit/miss/bytes receipts, which under std.materialization_ladder ARE the cache's economic justification rather than telemetry beside it: a provider is an optimization only when measured total serving cost is below recomputation, so if the receipts say it does not pay, the honest outcome is to say so. THE SEQUENCE IS NOT ARBITRARY: gunbc#10641 built the completion contract before any cache on the reasoning that a cache accelerates a contract and cannot supply one, and the remainder inherits that. TWO CEILINGS, unchanged and named rather than assumed: computed digests exist today ONLY through a shell realization over a file path (sha256sum), with no in-substrate bytes-to-digest, which one lane confirmed is a ceiling and not a blocker; and the digested file at commit must be the ORIGIN READBACK OWN OUTPUT, never a cache copy, or a valid computation over locally-sourced bytes launders a cache-read refusal into a confirmation. NO PEER MESH is a scope wall in the exit criterion, not an oversight. STORAGE ACCOUNTS ARE NO LONGER THE BLOCKER they were: both provider accounts now exist and a write-capable permission group is observed -- see storage-provider-accounts -- so what gates the origin is the executed mint, not the operator." },
}

data item_runner_canary: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "runner-canary",
  workstream: RunnerSoftware,
  milestone: M1InternalVertical,
  rank: 10,
  min_calendar_days: 3,
  execution_mode: AgentParallel,
  exit_criterion: "The control-plane canary, split from the oversized runner-path item (analyst rank-1 schedule risk): GitHub App + workflow_job webhook -> JIT ephemeral runner in a per-job microVM on the canary host -> one real job completes -> receipt with metered vCPU-minutes -> environment destroyed. Lifecycle hardening is runner-lifecycle (M2).",
  depends_on: no_deps,
  relates_to: ["strategy.ci_alpha"],
  status: InProgress { note: "ONE REAL JOB NOW COMPLETES: GitHub Actions run 34086151842 executed to success in a per-job microVM on the Mt. Collins host (gunbc#10733, main c9ef79fdeea) -- host boots its own EFI-stub kernel from BMC media, the jailer drops privilege (CapEff/Prm/Inh/Amb zero, NoNewPrivs=1, Seccomp=2) and execs Firecracker v1.16.1 in place, the guest runs under KVM on kernel 6.1.128 against a host on 6.12.108, and the runner registers from inside the guest. Containment was asserted BY THE JOB rather than trusted from the host receipt: a step opened /dev/tcp to the BMC controlling its own machine and required failure, and two observers with no shared instrument agree -- GitHub log plus the host guest-to-bmc rule carrying exactly 3 dropped packets, the SYN and its two retransmits. forward-default-drop stayed at ZERO across 567 established packets of runner-chosen traffic. Since then: two VMs on one host, and a 5832-file checkout running node v24.19.0 inside the guest. THREE CONJUNCTS REMAIN. (a) GitHub App to workflow_job webhook via fleet convergence -- the host currently asks for a JIT config at boot and THE ENDPOINT SERVING IT IS UNAUTHENTICATED, recorded in gunbc#10733 as a real gap rather than left in a shell script. (b) Receipt with metered vCPU-minutes -- not established. (c) Environment destroyed in the SAME TRANSACTION as the mint -- and zero-surviving-jailer is NOT evidence for it, because the stock jailer execs into Firecracker in place on the same PID; teardown must bind to the attempt launch/exec identity set including the BusyBox timeout watcher. The srv1 kvm-group gap no longer blocks this item: the canary executes on Mt. Collins, not srv1." },
}

data item_cohort_qualified: PlanItem = PlanItem {
  id: "cohort-qualified",
  workstream: AcquisitionMarketing,
  milestone: M1InternalVertical,
  rank: 10,
  min_calendar_days: 14,
  execution_mode: ExternalLead,
  exit_criterion: "STARTS NOW - November customers cannot be manufactured after October dogfood. THE DISCOVERY METHOD IS A SEARCH, WITH TWO CLAIMS ABOUT IT WITHDRAWN. Repositories running on a third-party CI provider are ENUMERABLE, because the provider label appears verbatim in .github/workflows, and their workflows are readable, so the arm64 and container questions below are answered by READING rather than by interviewing. What that label does NOT establish was overstated on first writing and is corrected here. It does not establish ARM: a provider offering both architectures selects by SUFFIXED labels, and only the arm-suffixed ones are evidence of an arm64 workload - an unsuffixed label is evidence of the opposite. It does not establish PAID SPEND either, because free tiers and credits exist, so willingness to pay stays an interview question rather than a search result. The residual interview is therefore LARGER than first claimed: org-owner availability, secrets and private registries, which deploy jobs must be excluded, and whether the account is actually paying. The requirement: 3-5 prospects identified and workload-qualified (arm64 compatibility, Docker/service-container needs, privileged-mode assumptions, private registries, secrets, excluded deploy jobs, job memory/disk ceilings, untrusted-PR behavior), org-owner availability confirmed, expected usage and dates committed.",
  depends_on: no_deps,
  relates_to: ["strategy.ci_alpha"],
  status: NotStarted,
}

